<Project>

    <!--
      The framework and the Jellyfin package version are the two halves of a server
      line, and this refuses the half-change: a line renamed without its pin moving,
      or a pin bumped past the line it is supposed to belong to.

      A framework this repository maps to no line, and a pin naming a package that
      does not support the framework, are both already refused during restore, by
      NU1015 and NU1202 respectively, before any target in this file runs. A second
      arm for either would be a guard that cannot be made to bite. What is left is
      the case NuGet cannot see, because both halves restore perfectly well and only
      disagree about which server the result is for.

      The check applies to projects that declare UsesJellyfinPackages, so a project
      with no business naming a server line is not asked to name one.
    -->
    <Target Name="RefuseAMismatchedServerLine"
            BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild"
            Condition="'$(UsesJellyfinPackages)' == 'true' and '$(TargetFramework)' != ''">

        <Error Condition="!$([System.String]::Copy('$(JellyfinPackageVersion)').StartsWith('$(JellyfinServerLine).'))"
               Text="Server line $(JellyfinServerLine) is pinned to Jellyfin package version $(JellyfinPackageVersion), which belongs to a different line. Framework $(TargetFramework), its line and its package version are changed together or not at all." />

    </Target>

    <!--
      Directory.Build.props reads the version out of build.yaml with a line match.
      A manifest whose version line moved, lost its quotes or gained a comma leaves
      that match empty, and an empty AssemblyVersion is not an error anywhere in the
      SDK: the build carries on and stamps 1.0.0.0, which is the number a project
      that never set one gets. So the failure this refuses is silent by default and
      arrives as an assembly whose version nobody in this repository chose.

      It fails the build rather than the test run because the wrong bytes are
      produced before any test reads them, and because the projects here that ship
      nothing are stamped from the same property and would otherwise carry the
      default without anything noticing.
    -->
    <Target Name="RefuseAVersionNothingRead"
            BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild">

        <Error Condition="'$(PluginVersion)' == ''"
               Text="No version could be read from $(PluginManifest). The reader in Directory.Build.props wants a line at column zero reading version: followed by two to four numeric parts in double quotes, and every assembly in this tree is stamped from what it finds." />

    </Target>

</Project>
